<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-hosted large language model (LLM) serving is emerging as a distinct category of Internet service, but we still know little about how these deployments appear and change on the public Internet.
We present a 365-day longitudinal measurement of exposed Ollama endpoints (port 11434) from February 2025 to February 2026, combining daily active probing with GeoIP/ASN enrichment, PTR and port-443 host observations, and survival analysis.
Across 362 observation days and approximately 4.8 million IP<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>day observations, 26.4% of the 152,137 cumulative IPs appear for a single day; across five selected CVEs, only 0.43–2.90% of below-fix IPs upgraded in place; the top five countries/regions account for over 70% of weighted observations; and cloud and hosting providers dominate the top ASNs.
These results characterize exposed Ollama as a structural exposure surface: persistent, growing, and heavily concentrated.
At the same time, old versions, common model choices, cloud and hosting ASNs, PTR categories, and TLS certificate patterns remain visible across the year, indicating recurring insecure deployment practices in cloud infrastructure and the potential reach of provider-level mitigation.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Internet measurement, exposed services, LLM infrastructure, Ollama, longitudinal analysis
</div><span id="id13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_cc-license"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">cc-license: </span>by</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-hosted large language model (LLM) serving has emerged as a distinct category of Internet service.
Ollama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2024</a>)</cite>, one of the most widely adopted of these frameworks, ships its REST API on port 11434 without built-in authentication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
Common deployment paths, including Docker images and tutorials for LAN or remote access, can bind the service to a public interface.
The result is that an Ollama instance is often directly reachable on the public Internet absent an external firewall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Industry scans have reported over 175,000 exposed instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernadett-Shapiro and Cutler, 2026</a>)</cite>, alongside smaller focused scans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tziakouris and Biasiotto, 2025</a>)</cite>, and multiple vulnerabilities have been disclosed (CVE-2024-37032 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiz Research, 2024</a>)</cite>, CVE-2024-39719 through CVE-2024-39722 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oligo Security, 2024</a>)</cite>, CNVD-2025-04094 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">NSFOCUS, 2025</a>)</cite>).
However, these counts tell us little about the underlying dynamics: existing analyses are overwhelmingly snapshot-based, leaving open basic questions about how long endpoints remain exposed, what software they run and how it evolves, and where and by whom these services are operated.
Longitudinal characterization of this emerging service category remains absent from the measurement literature.
Prior work on exposed services has focused on databases, cloud storage, and IoT devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Continella et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirian et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, while threatintelligence reports on LLM infrastructure typically span a single observation window <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tziakouris and Biasiotto, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernadett-Shapiro and Cutler, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GreyNoise Intelligence, 2026</a>)</cite>.
The community therefore lacks empirical answers to whether exposure is transient or persistent, whether the software ecosystem is stable or in flux, and whether mitigation can be targeted through a small number of infrastructure operators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a 365-day longitudinal measurement of exposed Ollama service endpoints from 2025-02-14 to 2026-02-13.
Using XMap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, 2025</a>)</cite>, we perform daily active probing of port 11434 across the full IPv4 address space and collect model inventories and version strings via the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/tags</span> and <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/version</span> endpoints.
Our multi-stage pipeline enriches responsive IPs with GeoIP/ASN mapping, performs PTR reverse DNS lookups with forward verification, probes port 443 for TLS certificates, and applies survival analysis to characterize endpoint lifetimes.
After excluding three days affected by collection failures, we retain 362 observation days and approximately 4.8 million IP<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>day observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using this dataset, we seek to address four research questions concerning the population of Internet-exposed Ollama services:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1 (Temporal Dynamics).</span>
What are the survival and churn characteristics of exposed Ollama endpoints?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2 (Software Ecosystem).</span>
How do model and version diversity evolve among exposed Ollama endpoints?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3 (Infrastructure Concentration).</span>
How concentrated are exposed Ollama endpoints across geographies and ASNs?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4 (Operational Characterization).</span>
What operational characteristics do exposed Ollama endpoints exhibit?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We find that the daily number of exposed Ollama endpoints rises through 2025-Q1, peaks in 2025-Q2, declines through 2025-Q3, and recovers from 2025-Q4 into 2026-Q1.
Over the full measurement window, the daily exposed population grows by 53.3% and never drops below its starting level.
IP-level survival has a large one-day spike (26.4% appear for a single day) and a persistent survivor population (10.3% are observed on at least 90 days).
The main pattern is broader than IP lifetime alone: new and old IPs repeatedly expose similar Ollama versions, model choices, cloud and hosting ASNs, PTR categories, and TLS certificate patterns.
The software ecosystem expands by 214% in unique models, while version lifetimes remain much longer than IP lifetimes (P50=114 days for versions, compared with 5 days for IPs).
Across five selected CVEs, in-place upgrade rates stay at only 0.43%–2.90%; patch-level improvement mainly comes from old IPs leaving and new IPs arriving with different versions.
Exposure is concentrated geographically and infrastructurally: the top five countries/regions account for about 70% of weighted observations, and the top five ASNs are all cloud or hosting providers.
We also probe the full population for PTR records and attempt TLS handshakes on port 443.
PTR records exist for 43.22% of cumulative IPs; among those, cloud and hosting providers together account for 56.69%.
TLS handshakes succeed on 14.44% of IPs, with Let’s Encrypt accounting for 49.39% of TLS-responsive IPs.
Together, these results characterize exposed Ollama as a recurring cloud deployment pattern with software, configuration, and provider-level dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span>
We make four contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Year-scale artifact.</span>
We release aggregate and de-identified data covering the 362-day observation window, together with the analysis and figure-generation pipeline, providing a public resource for studying exposed LLM serving infrastructure at Internet scale.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exposure baselines.</span>
We establish the first longitudinal characterization of exposed LLM serving infrastructure, covering IP survival, version survival, model diversity, geographic and ASN concentration, and operational characteristics.
These baselines enable cross-system comparison for future measurements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operational census.</span>
We conduct full-population PTR probing to classify exposed IPs with PTR-derived operational categories, supplemented by port-443 TLS probing.
Cloud and hosting providers account for 56.69% of PTR-classifiable IPs.
This replaces prior sample-based estimates with a full-population PTR-based operational taxonomy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remediation strategy.</span>
We combine infrastructure concentration with measured CVE remediation patterns to identify provider-level interventions that can reach a substantial share of exposed endpoints.
Tenant notification and upstream default changes offer concrete paths to reduce the exposure surface and improve security at scale.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
<a href="#S2" title="2. Background and Related Work ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> positions our work against related measurement literature.
<a href="#S3" title="3. Measurement Design ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> details the measurement design, pipeline, and data quality.
<a href="#S4" title="4. Temporal Dynamics ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S5" title="5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#S6" title="6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S7" title="7. Operational Characterization ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> present results for RQ1–RQ4 respectively.
<a href="#S8" title="8. Discussion ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> discusses implications and limitations, and <a href="#S9" title="9. Conclusion ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> concludes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background and Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we summarize Ollama’s deployment and attack surface and review related work on exposed-service measurement and AI serving infrastructure.</p>
</div>
