</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Ollama and Self-Hosted LLM Serving</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ollama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2024</a>)</cite> provides a lightweight HTTP-based API for running LLMs locally, exposing model management and inference endpoints on a single port (default 11434).
Its design prioritizes ease of adoption: a single-command install reaches running-model state, and the API ships without any built-in authentication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">NSFOCUS, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
The host install binds to <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">x.x.x.x</span> by default, but the official Docker image binds to <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">x.x.x.x:11434</span> via its standard <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">-p 11434:11434</span> recipe, and tutorials routinely instruct users to set <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">OLLAMA_HOST=x.x.x.x</span> for LAN or remote-client access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2026b</a>)</cite>.
Once any of these paths exposes the service to a public interface, an Internet-reachable instance becomes immediately discoverable by network scanners and accessible to any client.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The exposed API supports inference and embeddings as well as pulling, creating, copying, and deleting models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2026c</a>)</cite>.
An unauthenticated endpoint may therefore expose compute and model-management capabilities.
Ollama runs on systems ranging from a developer laptop to a cloud VM.
Commercial cloud platforms further lower deployment friction: Google Cloud documents Ollama deployment on GPU-enabled Cloud Run, AWS provides a one-click EC2 workflow that bundles Ollama with OpenWebUI, and DigitalOcean offers an Ollama/OpenWebUI 1-Click App through its Marketplace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google Cloud, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amazon Web Services, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">DigitalOcean, 2026</a>)</cite>.
Multiple vulnerabilities have also been disclosed, including remote code execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiz Research, 2024</a>)</cite>, file disclosure and denial-of-service <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oligo Security, 2024</a>)</cite>, and unauthorized access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">NSFOCUS, 2025</a>)</cite>, highlighting the security risks faced by exposed instances.
Industry reports have further documented exploitation attempts in the wild <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GreyNoise Intelligence, 2026</a>)</cite>.
The adoption dynamics of self-hosted LLM serving are thus linked to an emerging exposure pattern that warrants measurement study.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Exposed-Service Measurement</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large-scale surveys of exposed services constitute a long-running thread in Internet measurement.
Three strands are relevant here:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discovery.</span>
Tools such as ZMap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Durumeric et al., 2013</a>)</cite> support active Internet scanning, while Censys <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Durumeric et al., 2015</a>)</cite> and Shodan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shodan, 2009</a>)</cite> provide Internet-wide service indexes.
Together, they provide discovery infrastructure for exposure studies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Service-specific exposure.</span>
Subsequent work has characterized exposed databases and misconfigured services via decoy analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, cloud storage buckets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Continella et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and industrial control systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirian et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
Observation windows typically span from single snapshots to a few months, which bounds the visibility of persistence and churn.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Longitudinal misconfiguration.</span>
A body of work tracks exposed services over extended periods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Continella et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and demonstrates that misconfiguration is often persistent and that churn analysis exposes structural properties invisible to snapshot methods.
This longitudinal lens motivates our design.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these three strands establish the discovery infrastructure that makes our study feasible while also defining the gap we address: service-specific exposure studies rarely sustain observation beyond a few months, and the handful of longitudinal efforts confirm that short windows systematically undercount persistence and churn.
Our work applies the longitudinal lens to self-hosted LLM serving, a service class whose year-scale exposure dynamics remain unexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>AI Serving Infrastructure</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research has examined ML infrastructure from several angles, including GPU cluster configurations, inference API usage patterns, and model supply-chain risks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, alongside a broader survey of LLM security and privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Systematic longitudinal measurement of exposed LLM-serving endpoints remains absent from the academic literature.
The closest efforts are industry reports that may capture only a snapshot of exposure: Cisco Talos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tziakouris and Biasiotto, 2025</a>)</cite> documented exposed Ollama instances via a one-time Shodan query; SentinelOne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernadett-Shapiro and Cutler, 2026</a>)</cite> analyzed anonymized AI networks; and GreyNoise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GreyNoise Intelligence, 2026</a>)</cite> observed exploitation campaigns through its sensor network.
These reports contribute situational awareness, and they share three methodological limitations that our study addresses:</p>
<ul id="S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Snapshot bias.</span>
A single timestamp cannot separate transient misconfiguration from persistent deployment, nor can it quantify churn or survival.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Indirect sourcing.</span>
Reliance on third-party indices (Shodan, honeypot telemetry) ties observations to source indexing cadence, and inherited biases cannot be audited.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope fragmentation.</span>
Individual reports cover a single dimension (count, exploitation, anonymity), and no study combines temporal, ecosystem, infrastructure, and operational analysis in a unified frame.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These industry reports outline the exposed surface.
They cannot quantify its temporal dynamics, software changes, concentration, or operational profile.
Our study fills these gaps through a unified longitudinal design with direct first-party scanning and multi-dimensional enrichment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Adjacent serving frameworks include vLLM, LocalAI, and Text Generation Inference, which also provide HTTP APIs and container-based deployment paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">vLLM Project, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">LocalAI, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hugging Face, 2026</a>)</cite>.
We mention them as relevant alternatives; differences in binding, authentication, and deployment channels mean that our Ollama measurements should not be generalized to them directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work is, to our knowledge, the first year-scale longitudinal measurement of exposed LLM serving infrastructure.
<a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2. Background and Related Work ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> positions our study against the major strands above; we differ on four axes: (i) <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">temporal depth</span>: 362 observed days, far exceeding the snapshot or short-window norm for this service class; (ii) <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">direct active probing</span>: daily first-party scans of port 11434 provide primary observations with response-level enrichment; (iii) <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multi-dimensional scope</span>: a unified analysis of temporal dynamics (RQ1), software ecosystem (RQ2), geographic and infrastructure concentration (RQ3), and operational characterization (RQ4); and (iv) <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">survival analysis</span>: IP-, model-, and version-level lifetime distributions that expose both a large one-day spike and a persistent survivor population in the same exposure surface.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Measurement Design</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the measurement design underlying our year-long study of exposed Ollama services.
We describe the observation setup, processing pipeline, data-quality controls, and analysis metrics.</p>
</div>
