</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Model and Version Turnover</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next examine how this diversity is distributed across the exposed population and how the leading models and versions change over time.
<a href="#S5.T3" title="In 5.1. Model and Version Diversity ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the most common models, model families, and versions.
For software rankings, we count observation events over time: a model contributes once for each IP-day on which it is reported, while a version contributes once for each parseable IP-day version observation.
Model families are extracted as the prefix before the first colon in the model identifier (e.g., <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama3.2:latest</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama</span>).
The top model family is <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama</span> with 8.65M aggregate observations (46.3%), followed by <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qwen2</span> with 3.9M (21.1%).
At the individual model level, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">smollm2:135m</span> leads with 1.49M observations (8.0%), and the top-10 models account for only 34.0% of all model-day events.
Among versions, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span> leads with 288k observations (6.5%), reflecting the persistent population older than the Probllama fix analyzed in <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.
Notably, model names ending in <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">:latest</span> are mutable aliases, which we retain as reported in the analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across the year, model and version rankings exhibit different turnover patterns.
At the model level, the leader within the global top-10 changes quickly before the monthly top slot stabilizes in 2025-07 on <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">smollm2:135m</span> (<a href="#acmlabel4" title="In 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
The model distribution remains broad: even after <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">smollm2:135m</span> reaches rank 1, thousands of less-deployed model identifiers remain visible.
At the version level, monthly rankings within the global top-10 reveal a split between an early release-driven phase and a later old-version-heavy phase (<a href="#acmlabel5" title="In 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
From 2025-02 through 2025-09, the leader within this set tracks Ollama’s release cycle, changing every one to two months: 0.5.7 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.5.13 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.6.2 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.6.5 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.9.0 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.9.6.
From 2025-10 onward, version <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span>, a pre-Probllama release from 2024, leads this set while ranking second or third across the full version population, with 17,900–37,202 observations per month.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Model and Version Active Days</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then compare IP, model, and version active-day distributions to determine whether software identifiers remain visible in the population longer than individual IP addresses (<a href="#acmlabel6" title="In 5.1. Model and Version Diversity ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
These active-day counts measure how long an identifier remains visible across the population, irrespective of which endpoint reports it on each day.
Among 26,087 distinct model identifiers, 10.8% appear for a single day.
The median model active-day count is 18 days (P50), with P90 at 182 days.
Several top models appear on 360 out of 362 days, including <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama3:latest</span>, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mistral:latest</span>, and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">smollm2:135m</span>; these models form a stable core within the model layer.
Versions are even more persistent: among 313 distinct versions, the median active-day count is 114 days and 39.3% appear on 180 days or more.
Measured by median active-day counts, persistence increases across the three types: IPs remain visible for a few days (5 days), model identifiers for a few weeks (18 days), and platform versions for several months (114 days).
In short, IPs come and go quickly, models change at a moderate pace, and platform versions move slowly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A deploy-and-leave pattern.</span>
Taken together, the model and version observations suggest that some exposed Ollama endpoints are deployed with common model choices and receive limited subsequent modification.
At the model level, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">smollm2:135m</span>, listed at 271 MB in the Ollama Library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama, 2026d</a>)</cite>, rises in 2025-Q1, dips in mid-2025, and reaches <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}6.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}6.4</annotation></semantics></math>k endpoints/day by 2026-01 (<a href="#acmlabel4" title="In 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Its daily count shows a moderate positive correlation with the daily alive-IP count (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}0.46</annotation></semantics></math> after excluding low-coverage days).
Other widely deployed models follow different temporal patterns, indicating that this relationship is not uniform across the model ecosystem.
At the version level, the continued prominence of <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span> shows that older software remains in the exposed population long after newer releases appear.
Combined with high daily IP churn and the 26.4% one-day IP share (<a href="#S4" title="4. Temporal Dynamics ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), these patterns suggest an exposed population containing short-lived deployments with common model choices alongside persistent old-version groups.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel7">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.07115v1/fig_cve_a.svg" id="S5.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/262;" width="476" height="262"></object>
