</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S5.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Per-IP transitions across CVE-fix cutoffs.<span id="S5.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Sankey panels show IP-level states from a baseline <math id="S5.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the final observation day <math id="S5.F7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=2026-02-13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2026</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=2026-02-13</annotation></semantics></math> for five version cutoffs.
For the three 2024 cutoffs (<math id="S5.F7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.34, <math id="S5.F7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.47, <math id="S5.F7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.3.15), <math id="S5.F7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first day with version data (2025-02-23), since their fixes predate the observation window; for the two 2025 cutoffs (<math id="S5.F7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.6.8, <math id="S5.F7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.12.4), <math id="S5.F7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fix release date.
States are below-cutoff, fixed, and absent; ribbon width is proportional to the number of IPs in each transition.
“Vuln.” in the panels is shorthand for a version below the cutoff.</span></span><span id="acmlabel7" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Five Sankey panels arranged in a 3+2 grid, one per CVE-fix cutoff (&lt;0.1.34 Probllama, &lt;0.1.47 ZipSlip RCE, &lt;0.3.15 GGUF DoS in the top row; &lt;0.6.8 token theft, &lt;0.12.4 auth bypass in the bottom row, centered).
Each panel shows three vertically stacked nodes (vulnerable, fixed, absent) on the left and on the right, connected by ribbons whose width is proportional to the count of IPs in that transition.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.07115v1/fig_patch_adoption.svg" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/178;" width="476" height="178" aria-label="Line chart over time for five Ollama version cutoffs.
Each line reports the daily share below one cutoff among parseable version observations."><p>Line chart over time for five Ollama version cutoffs.
Each line reports the daily share below one cutoff among parseable version observations.</p></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>. </span><span id="S5.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">CVE-fix adoption over time.<span id="S5.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Daily share of parseable Ollama versions below each of five CVE-fix cutoffs.
Grey bands mark low-coverage days (<a href="#S3" title="3. Measurement Design ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></span><span id="acmlabel8" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Line chart over time for five Ollama version cutoffs.
Each line reports the daily share below one cutoff among parseable version observations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>CVE Patch Adoption</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple vulnerabilities have been disclosed in Ollama, making remediation an important dimension of the exposed software ecosystem.
Ollama’s simple versioning scheme and lack of separate patched legacy versions make the <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/version</span> string an observable indicator of potential CVE status: an IP whose version parses as <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,v</annotation></semantics></math> is classified as below the version at which the CVE was fixed.
Notably, this classification does not establish exploitability under an endpoint’s runtime configuration.
Using this version-based classification, we analyze remediation across five selected CVEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We select five of the 21 Ollama daemon CVEs disclosed during the observation window.
Each retained cutoff meets four requirements:</p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the fixed version is visible in <span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/version</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the below-fix group covers at least 5% of observations;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the selected cutoffs span the disclosure timeline; and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the set covers different vulnerability types.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We exclude the rejected duplicate CVE-2024-7773, the small CVE-2024-28224 group (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 0.1.29, 0.5% of version observations), CVEs with no recorded fix version (CVE-2025-1975, CVE-2025-44779), and late-disclosed CVEs such as CVE-2025-15514.
CVEs with a shared fix version produce the same below-fix population and remediation curve, so we analyze each shared version cutoff once.
The Appendix gives the full cutoff-selection rules (<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D CVE Coverage and Cutoff Selection ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).
The five resulting cutoffs are: <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.34 (CVE-2024-37032, Probllama RCE), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.47 (CVE-2024-45436, ZipSlip RCE), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.3.15 (CVE-2024-39719, file-existence disclosure), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.6.8 (CVE-2025-51471, cross-domain token exposure), and <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.12.4 (CVE-2025-63389, authentication bypass).
The <span id="S5.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/version</span> endpoint returns a parseable string for 87.3–99.0% of the daily alive population, giving a high-coverage denominator throughout the year. Using the parseable version observations, we then examine the below-cutoff population for each CVE through IP-level transitions and aggregate trends over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#acmlabel7" title="In 5.3. Model and Version Active Days ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> traces each IP from a baseline <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=2026-02-13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2026</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=2026-02-13</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the 2025 cutoffs, <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fix-release date; for the 2024 cutoffs, <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first day with version data because the fixes predate our observation window.
The percentage denominator is the set of IPs with a parseable below-cutoff version at <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; an IP without a parseable observation at either endpoint is classified as absent.
Across all five cutoffs, only 0.43–2.90% of IPs below the cutoff at <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> upgrade in place by <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; 30–65% disappear from the population; and 882–1,796 IPs first appear <em id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">after</em> <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with versions older than the relevant fix, adding new below-cutoff observations to the population.
The same pattern holds across five CVEs spanning our measurement window.
Exposed-Ollama remediation is driven mainly by population turnover: even among long-lived IPs that persist through the observation window, at most 2.90% upgrade in place, indicating that persistently exposed instances are rarely patched.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel9">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.07115v1/fig_geo_top_countries.svg" id="S5.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/432;" width="476" height="432"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F9.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Top countries/regions.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.07115v1/fig_geo_top_asns.svg" id="S5.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/432;" width="476" height="432"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F9.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Top ASNs.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>. </span><span id="S5.F9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Top country/region and ASN shares.<span id="S5.F9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Donut panels show weighted endpoint-observation shares for the top countries/regions and autonomous systems, with remaining observations grouped as “Other”.</span></span><span id="acmlabel9" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Two donut charts showing the top five countries/regions plus other, and the top five ASNs plus other.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2609.07115v1/fig_world_map.png" id="S5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="World map with fixed-size country/region markers whose colors encode weighted IP-day observations on a logarithmic scale, showing the largest concentrations in East Asia, Europe, and North America.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>. </span><span id="S5.F10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Global distribution of exposed endpoints.<span id="S5.F10.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Geolocated exposed IPs by country/region.
Marker color uses a logarithmic scale of weighted IP-day observations.</span></span><span id="acmlabel10" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">World map with fixed-size country/region markers whose colors encode weighted IP-day observations on a logarithmic scale, showing the largest concentrations in East Asia, Europe, and North America.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2609.07115v1/fig_as_country_heatmap.png" id="S5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307" alt="A heatmap with AS labels on rows and country/region labels on columns showing concentration intensity.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>. </span><span id="S5.F11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">AS <math id="S5.F11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> country/region concentration heatmap.<span id="S5.F11.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Weighted endpoint observations by autonomous system and country/region.
Cell color uses a logarithmic scale.</span></span><span id="acmlabel11" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A heatmap with AS labels on rows and country/region labels on columns showing concentration intensity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#acmlabel8" title="In 5.3. Model and Version Active Days ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the below-cutoff share of the parseable population for each cutoff across the full year.
Unlike <a href="#acmlabel7" title="In 5.3. Model and Version Active Days ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, its denominator is all parseable version observations on each adequately covered day.
Among the three cutoffs whose fixes predate the observation window, Probllama (<math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.34) rises by 1.39 pp, ZipSlip (<math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.1.47) declines by 0.13 pp, and GGUF (<math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.3.15) declines from 21.43% to 11.10%.
The two 2025 cutoffs, in contrast, decline after their fix dates: <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.6.8 (token theft, CVE-2025-51471) falls from 55.32% to 23.65% in seven months, and <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\,</annotation></semantics></math>0.12.4 (auth bypass, CVE-2025-63389) falls from 60.98% to 41.04% in two months.
These declining curves resemble the post-disclosure adoption pattern reported for Heartbleed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Durumeric et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, but the IP-level transitions in <a href="#acmlabel7" title="In 5.3. Model and Version Active Days ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reveal a different mechanism: at most 2.90% of below-cutoff IPs upgrade in place.
Population turnover drives the decline: newly arriving IPs carry later versions, diluting the below-cutoff share as older below-cutoff IPs exit.
Probllama is the only cutoff that drifts slightly upward, from 7.88% to 9.27%.
This rise is driven by version <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span>, the largest single version across the year and a top-three monthly version in the final five months.
We therefore argue that, for high-churn deployments, aggregate decline is a weak proxy for operator patching: it conflates population turnover with in-place upgrades, while arriving or reappearing IPs reporting older versions can further slow or reverse the decline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, the software ecosystem is rapidly diversifying: daily unique model counts increase by 214%, daily unique version counts by 62.5%, and 313 distinct version strings appear across the year.
The version head reflects both new Ollama releases and persistent old-version populations, most visibly <span id="S5.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span>, the largest single version across the year.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we examine exposed Ollama services across geographies and network infrastructure to characterize their distribution patterns and levels of concentration.</p>
</div>
