Each row sums to 100%.</span></span><span id="acmlabel12" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Two-panel figure: (a) Horizontal bar chart with paired bars for six PTR-present deployment categories.
(b) 100\% horizontal stacked bar chart with six survival buckets and seven colored segments per row representing the PTR-based deployment categories.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Country/Region-Level Concentration</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure geographic concentration across all 152,137 cumulative exposed IPs using weighted endpoint observations: each IP is weighted by the number of days it appears during the measurement window.
Therefore, long-lived exposed endpoints contribute more than one-day endpoints.
Under this IP-day weighting, exposure is concentrated in a small set of countries/regions (<a href="#acmlabel9" title="In 5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
China (CN) accounts for 29.09% of weighted endpoint observations, followed by the United States (US) at 21.65%, Germany (DE) at 9.06%, France (FR) at 7.43%, and Hong Kong (HK) at 2.82%.
The top-5 countries/regions together hold 70.1% of all observations and the top-10 hold 81.1%, although 157 countries/regions appear in the dataset.
The country/region-level HHI of 0.1500 sits at the conventional threshold for moderate concentration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rhoades, 1993</a>)</cite>.
<a href="#acmlabel10" title="In 5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> plots the global distribution; the densest cells fall in East Asia, Western Europe, and North America.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>ASN-Level Concentration</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the ASN level, exposure shifts from geographic concentration to provider concentration: the head is dominated by cloud and hosting networks, but the distribution extends across 4,465 distinct ASNs (<a href="#acmlabel9" title="In 5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
The top ASN is AS37963 (Alibaba/Hangzhou), with 8.02% of weighted observations.
The next four providers are Hetzner (6.05%), Tencent (4.17%), Contabo (3.95%), and HE/Linode (3.89%).
The top-10 ASNs together account for 42.1% of observations across 4,465 distinct ASNs (HHI = 0.0243, low at the ASN level despite moderate country/region-level concentration).
The top five ASNs are all cloud or hosting providers, consistent with server-side LLM deployments on managed infrastructure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We next combine the two dimensions to identify which provider–geography pairs carry the largest exposure shares (<a href="#acmlabel11" title="In 5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
The strongest pair is AS37963 <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> CN with a weighted count of 384,739, the largest single geographic cell in the dataset.
The contrast between moderate geographic HHI (0.1500) and low ASN-level HHI (0.0243) clarifies where concentration appears: exposure is geographically clustered, but within those geographies it is distributed across many network operators.
The cross-analysis therefore identifies the specific (provider, jurisdiction) cells where notification or remediation efforts would have the highest leverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Cloud-Provider Perspective</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain a provider-level view, we group top ASNs operated by the same cloud or hosting provider and aggregate their weighted endpoint observations (<a href="#S6.T4" title="In 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
This re-aggregation captures providers that operate multiple ASNs and would otherwise appear as separate entities in the ASN-level analysis.
The top-10 providers together account for 46.69% of all weighted endpoint observations, and the top-5 alone cover 33.51%.
The provider-centric view points to a mitigation pathway that country/region- and ASN-level statistics alone obscure: coordinated changes to deployment defaults, together with tenant-facing exposure alerts from a handful of cloud operators, could reach a substantial fraction of the exposure surface.
<a href="#S8" title="8. Discussion ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> develops this mitigation implication in detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In all, the multi-level view across country/region, ASN, and re-aggregated providers reveals a consistent structure: exposure is concentrated geographically and distributed across many individual ASNs, while a substantial share remains associated with a relatively small set of cloud and hosting providers.
The re-aggregated provider lens therefore shows that a small number of cloud operators can reach a substantial share of the surface, making coordinated provider-level action a practical mitigation pathway.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Operational Characterization</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we examine the operational context of exposed Ollama endpoints by identifying hosting patterns and examining the configuration and maintenance of co-located HTTPS services.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>PTR-based Deployment Categorization</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PTR records provide a contextual deployment signal for exposed endpoints (<a href="#S6.F12.sf1" title="In Figure 12 ‣ 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(a)</span></a>).
We queried PTR records for all 152,137 cumulative IPs.
PTR records are present for 43.22% (65,747) of IPs, while 56.78% (86,390) return no PTR record.
Among PTR-present IPs, 49.61% (32,614) pass forward verification, meaning the A/AAAA record returned by the PTR-discovered hostname resolves back to the original IP; this covers 21.44% of the full IP population.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We classify each PTR-present IP into one of six operational categories (<a href="#S6.F12.sf1" title="In Figure 12 ‣ 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(a)</span></a>) using cascading priority rules: the first matching rule determines the category.
The rules are applied in order:</p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hyperscale-cloud TLD whitelists,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VPS-hosting brand TLD whitelists,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
