<ul id="S7.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S7.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Unknown denotes blank or placeholder issuer values.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Other combines all remaining issuer strings.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The largest share falls under cloud and hosting infrastructure: hyperscale cloud accounts for 12.97% of all IPs (30.01% of PTR-present), VPS hosting for 9.89% (22.88%), and generic VPS naming for 1.64% (3.79%).
Cloud and hosting together cover 24.49% of the full population (56.69% of PTR-present), consistent with the provider-level concentration observed in <a href="#S6" title="6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Residential / dynamic ISPs account for only 6.27% (14.51% of PTR-present) and named telcos for 3.31% (7.65%); together they cover 9.58% of all IPs.
A further 9.14% of IPs fall into custom domains (typically self-hosted DNS for a long-lived service), and 56.78% return no PTR record at all.
<a href="#S6.T5" title="In 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> lists the representative domain patterns that anchor each classification category; the full whitelists and regular expressions are documented in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C PTR Classification Details ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>TLS Certificate Characterization</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some exposed Ollama endpoints may be co-located with a service or reverse proxy on port 443.
In such cases, the presented TLS certificates provide a co-hosting signal and reveal operational characteristics such as certificate validity, issuer, and signature algorithm.
To examine this, we attempted one TLS handshake on port 443 for each cumulative IP.
Of all 152,137 IPs, 14.44% (21,963) completed a TLS handshake.
Among these TLS-responsive IPs, 9.68% (2,127) had expired certificates by the time of probing.
Let’s Encrypt accounts for 49.39% of TLS-responsive IPs, followed by auto-generated Traefik certificates (13.27%) and additional commercial CAs (DigiCert, TrustAsia, ZeroSSL, Sectigo, Amazon, Cloudflare), which points to widespread use of automated certificate issuance and proxy-based deployment stacks.
Signature algorithms are led by RSA-SHA256 (66.58%) and ECDSA-SHA384 (27.39%), with legacy algorithms like MD5-RSA (3.53%) and SHA1-RSA (0.24%) remaining visible.
The certificate issuer mix also differs across PTR categories (<a href="#S7.T7" title="In 7.2. TLS Certificate Characterization ‣ 7. Operational Characterization ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Amazon-issued certificates account for 24.4% of TLS-responsive hyperscale cloud IPs, whereas Let’s Encrypt (52.4%) and Traefik (30.5%) dominate VPS hosting IPs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, Only a minority of exposed Ollama IPs also respond to TLS handshakes on port 443 (14.44%), and the responsive subset combines widespread automated certificate provisioning with a smaller tail of expired certificates and legacy signature algorithms. Differences across PTR categories further show that these operational profiles vary with hosting context.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T7" class="ltx_table">

