</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Survival and Deployment Type</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we group IPs into six lifetime buckets based on the number of days observed and compare PTR-category distributions across them (<a href="#S6.F12.sf2" title="In Figure 12 ‣ 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(b)</span></a>).
The distributions shift substantially across IP-lifetime buckets, separating short-lived and long-lived infrastructure profiles (<a href="#S6.F12.sf2" title="In Figure 12 ‣ 6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(b)</span></a>).
The 1-day bucket (<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=40,221) is dominated by hyperscale-cloud IPs (27.3%) and no-PTR IPs (49.8%); residential ISPs contribute 8.5%, while VPS hosting contributes only 3.8%.
The <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>181-day bucket (<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=6,562) inverts this: hyperscale-cloud share collapses to 5.2%, residential ISPs to 1.1%, while VPS hosting rises to 20.7% and custom-domain IPs to 12.3%.
The shift is consistent across the lifetime buckets, with the hyperscale share decreasing at each step and the VPS-hosting share increasing from 3.8% in the one-day bucket to 20.7% in the <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>181-day bucket.
These trends show that IP churn is concentrated in particular infrastructure types.
Short-lived hyperscale cloud endpoints and more persistent VPS-hosted or custom-domain endpoints help explain how rapid daily churn can coexist with recurring hosting patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the PTR and TLS results reveal multiple operational profiles within the exposed population.
Transient hyperscale cloud endpoints, persistent VPS-hosted services, and heterogeneous HTTPS configurations show that Ollama exposure is sustained across several deployment contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we examine the recurring exposure pattern and practical remediation options.
We then consider implications for similar self-hosted AI services and the study’s main limitations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1. </span>Exposure Pattern</h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The longitudinal results reveal an exposed Ollama population characterized by rapid IP turnover, recurring software distributions, and concentrated hosting infrastructure.
The observed IP population changes rapidly, with 26.4% of IPs appearing on only one day and roughly 775 IPs entering and 761 IPs leaving the population each day (<a href="#S4" title="4. Temporal Dynamics ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Despite rapid IP turnover, the exposed population retains a recognizable software structure.
Model and version identifiers remain visible longer than individual IP addresses, and <span id="S8.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0.1.33</span>, a release from 2024, ranks as the largest single version across the year and among the top three monthly versions in the final five months (<a href="#S5" title="5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
The exposed population therefore changes at the IP level while continuing to reproduce common model choices and substantial old-version groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Exposure is also concentrated within a relatively small number of infrastructure providers.
The top 10 providers carry 46.69% of all weighted observations, while hyperscale cloud and VPS hosting together account for 56.69% of PTR-classifiable IPs (<a href="#S6" title="6. Geographic and Infrastructure Concentration ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S7" title="7. Operational Characterization ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
This concentration places a substantial share of the exposed surface within reach of framework- and provider-level intervention.
Across the five selected CVE cutoffs, at most 2.90% of below-cutoff IPs upgrade in place (<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. CVE Patch Adoption ‣ 5. Software Ecosystem Evolution ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).
The limited transition rate shows that aggregate software change is shaped primarily by departures and later arrivals, with little observed upgrading among continuing IPs.
Together, rapid turnover, recurring software profiles, provider concentration, and limited in-place upgrading characterize an exposed surface that is continually renewed while retaining its underlying software and hosting structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.2. </span>Remediation Strategy</h3>

<div id="S8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The recurring nature of this exposure pattern points to supply-side mitigation as the higher-leverage path.
Rapid IP turnover limits the reach of operator-by-operator remediation, while persistent software profiles and provider concentration create opportunities for intervention by framework developers and hosting providers.
A one-time notification campaign targeting the currently observed population would provide only limited coverage over time, given the rapid turnover of exposed instances and potential changes in the operators behind them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These dynamics point to supply-side action by both framework developers and hosting providers.
First, framework developers can pair remote-access guidance with authentication or private-network controls and make version health more visible.
Second, cloud and hosting providers can detect publicly reachable port 11434 tenants and issue targeted notifications; the top-10 providers account for 46.69% of weighted observations, showing why this approach can reach a substantial share of the observed surface.
Operators can complement these measures by blocking direct public access to port 11434, keeping Ollama bound to localhost or a private interface, and exposing remote access through an authenticated HTTPS reverse proxy on port 443.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.3. </span>Implications and Outlook</h3>

<div id="S8.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ollama’s common deployment paths can make the service publicly accessible, while exposed endpoints exhibit rapid growth, persistent cloud-hosted exposure, and very low in-place upgrade rates.
Together, these patterns suggest limited operator maintenance.
Similar risks can arise in other self-hosted AI serving tools, such as vLLM, LocalAI, and Text Generation Inference, because they use similar deployment paths: container images, quick-start commands, cloud templates, and developer-oriented defaults <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">vLLM Project, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">LocalAI, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hugging Face, 2026</a>)</cite>.
Docker’s port-publishing model makes this path security-relevant: publishing a container port without a host address binds it on all host interfaces by default, potentially making the service reachable from outside the host <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Docker, 2026</a>)</cite>.
These paths make it easy to create an Internet-facing service quickly, and they also make repeated exposure likely when users copy the same deployment pattern.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An interesting direction is to examine whether similar exposure and maintenance patterns emerge across other AI serving systems and, if so, to identify the common factors that may contribute to them.
As self-hosted AI services expand, we expect deployment defaults and provider-side controls to play an increasingly important role in determining how these services are exposed, secured, and maintained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.4. </span>Threats to Validity</h3>

<div id="S8.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several aspects of our measurement design may constrain our scope and interpretation of the reported findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Measurement scope</span>: our single-vantage measurement covers public IPv4 addresses reachable on Ollama’s default port, and all results use the IP address as the unit of observation.
Deployments behind NATs, firewalls, or authenticated reverse proxies and those using IPv6 or non-default ports fall outside this scope.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal coverage</span>: the 365-day window contains three missing scan days, leaving 362 observed days.
Eleven of these observed days have low coverage and are excluded from rate-based metrics; time-series figures mark them with grey bands (<a href="#S3" title="3. Measurement Design ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enrichment timing</span>: GeoIP/ASN, PTR, and port-443 observations were collected once after the longitudinal window and applied as fixed labels or snapshots.
They cannot reconstruct changes during the year, and GeoIP/ASN mappings may contain residual errors for cloud-hosted IPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poese et al., 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these limitations, we believe our core findings are robust, and we restrict our claims to the observational scope of the dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented a 365-day longitudinal measurement of exposed Ollama LLM endpoints at Internet scale.
Across 152,137 cumulative IPs and 362 daily observations, exposed Ollama deployments exhibit rapid IP-level churn while retaining stable structure in software versions, model use, geographic concentration, provider infrastructure, and organizational identity.
These findings suggest that exposed self-hosted LLM services are a persistent cloud-hosted pattern for which upstream-safe defaults and provider-side alerts are likely to be more effective than host-by-host remediation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.acknowledgements1" class="ltx_acknowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_acknowledgements">Acknowledgements.</h6>
We thank the anonymous reviewers for their constructive feedback and thoughtful suggestions.
We also thank our shepherd for careful guidance throughout the revision process.
