<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Ethics</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We designed our study to minimize its impact on the services we observe while producing findings that support defensive awareness of a rapidly growing service category.
We follow established ethical guidelines for Internet-scale measurement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bailey et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Partridge and Allman, 2016</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Measurement burden.</span>
Follow-up probing issues at most one HTTP GET to each of <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/tags</span> and <span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/api/version</span> per responsive IP; version collection began on 2025-02-23.
The measurement activity is limited to metadata retrieval and excludes inference requests, state changes, private-path enumeration, prompt submission, and model-weight retrieval.
PTR probing issues one reverse-DNS query per IP and performs forward A/AAAA verification when a PTR record is present; TLS probing attempts one handshake per IP.
Scan rates are bounded by XMap’s default pacing to avoid congestion on any individual AS.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data handling and release.</span>
All results reported in the paper are aggregated statistics (counts, proportions, concentration indices, survival distributions).
Tables, figures, and released artifacts contain aggregate statistics only.
The public artifact release accompanying this paper contains (i) per-day aggregated counts, (ii) model and version frequency tables, and (iii) country/region- and ASN-level shares; raw per-IP records are retained internally under access control and remain internal.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harm consideration.</span>
Exposure of Ollama endpoints has already been documented in multiple industry reports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tziakouris and Biasiotto, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernadett-Shapiro and Cutler, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GreyNoise Intelligence, 2026</a>)</cite>; our contribution is methodological longitudinal characterization of an already documented exposure surface.
We restrict claims to exposure and operational characterization.
Where our findings point to mitigations, we frame them at the level of upstream defaults and provider-scale policy, consistent with responsible-measurement practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Responsible disclosure.</span>
Our study identified publicly reachable Ollama deployments and did not uncover a new software vulnerability; consequently, no vendor-coordinated vulnerability disclosure was required.
All probes used read-only endpoints and avoided exploitation or service modification.
We describe the measurement methodology for reproducibility while withholding individual IP addresses and raw per-IP records.
The released artifacts contain only aggregate results and provide no reusable target list.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Generative AI Disclosure</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In accordance with the ACM IMC 2026 generative AI policy, we disclose the use of generative AI tools in the preparation of this manuscript.
Claude Code 2.1.121 was used for the following purposes: (i) refining scripts for figure generation; (ii) polishing LaTeX formatting; and (iii) language refinement of draft text.
All AI-generated outputs were reviewed, verified, and edited by the authors, who bear full responsibility for the scientific accuracy and integrity of the final manuscript.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>PTR Classification Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S7" title="7. Operational Characterization ‣ Ollama in the Wild: A Longitudinal Measurement of Exposed Ollama LLM Endpoints at Internet Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> classifies each PTR-present IP into one of six operational categories using cascading priority rules.
The full whitelists and regular expressions are documented below for reproducibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cascade order.</span>
Rules are applied in the order listed; the first match determines the category.</p>
</div>
